class Solution {
public:
    int largestRectangleArea(vector<int>& heights) {
        int n = heights.size();
        stack<int> st;
        int maxArea = 0;
        
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            int h = (i == n ? 0 : heights[i]);
            while (!st.empty() && h < heights[st.top()]) {
                int height = heights[st.top()];
                st.pop();
                int width = st.empty() ? i : (i - st.top() - 1);
                maxArea = max(maxArea, height * width);
            }
            st.push(i);
        }
        return maxArea;
    }
};

**Intuition:**  
Use a monotonic increasing stack to find the nearest smaller bars.  
For each bar, calculate the maximum rectangle where it is the shortest bar.

**Algorithm:**  
Traverse the histogram and maintain indices in an increasing stack.  
When a smaller bar appears, pop elements and calculate their rectangle area.  
Track the maximum area found.

**Complexity:**  
Time: **O(n)**  
Space: **O(n)**
